        "case_id": "gp007",
        "category": "golden",
        "question": "What is the formula for scaled dot-product attention, and how does it compare to additive attention?",
        "answer": "- Scaled dot‑product attention computes dot products between queries and keys, divides by √dₖ, applies softmax, and multiplies by values: Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(QKᵀ/√dₖ) V [chunk_1][chunk_2]\n- Dot‑product attention is the same as scaled dot‑product attention except for the scaling factor 1/√dₖ [chunk_1]\n- Additive attention computes query‑key compatibility with a feed‑forward network that has a single hidden layer [chunk_1]\n- Dot‑product attention is faster and more space‑efficient because it can be implemented with optimized matrix multiplication [chunk_2]\n- For small dₖ the two mechanisms perform similarly; for larger dₖ additive attention outperforms dot‑product attention without scaling [chunk_2]\n- Scaling the dot products by 1/√dₖ counteracts large‑magnitude dot products that push softmax into regions with very small gradients [chunk_2]",
